   102	## 2.4. Subtyping MDD based on individual deviations
   103	
   104	To  discern  MDD  subtypes  characterized  by  significant  deviation patterns  in  degree  centrality,  we  employed  a  data-driven  k-means clustering algorithm (Fig. 1E). Specifically, we leveraged the deviation maps of all MDD patients as the clustering features, with the distance between any two patients defined as the Euclidean distance calculated from  their  respective  deviation  maps.  This  approach  enabled  us  to identify  patient  clusters  sharing  similar  degree  centrality  deviation patterns. To refine the clustering performance, we adjusted the number of  clusters  (k = 2,  3, … ,  10)  and  implemented  the  Silhouette  Index (Rousseeuw, 1987) alongside the Calinski-Harabasz Index (Cali ´ nski and Harabasz,  1974)  as  evaluation  metrics  to  assess  the  clustering  effect across varying cluster counts. During the k-means clustering process, we enhanced its robustness and ensured the reliability of clustering outcomes  by  setting  the  maximum  number  of  iterations  to  10,000  and repeating  the  calculations  1000  times.  This  method  allowed  us  to ascertain the optimal number of clusters that best represented significant degree centrality deviation patterns among MDD patients.
   105	
   106	After  distinguishing  subtypes,  we  conducted  further  analyses  to compare  their  demographic  profiles  and  clinical  variables  (Fig.  1E). Using a similar approach, we generated spatial overlap maps specific to each  subtype,  portraying  the  percentages  of  individuals  exhibiting positive or negative outliers in various regions. We then compared these subtype-specific maps with the overall spatial overlap maps to investigate  whether  the  distribution  of  outliers  in  brain  regions  was  more consistent  within  MDD  subtypes  than  across  the  entire  MDD  patient population.
   107	
   108	To  delve  deeper  into  the  disparities  in  deviation  patterns  among MDD  subtypes,  we  performed  a  comparative  analysis  at  both  the regional  and  network  levels  (Fig.  1E).  At  the  regional  level,  we computed and compared the mean deviation maps for each subtype. At the network level, we adopted the seven brain networks defined by the Yeo  atlas,  including  the  visual  (VIS),  sensorimotor  (SMN),  dorsal attention (DAN), ventral attention (VAN), limbic (LIM), frontoparietal (FPN), and default mode (DMN) networks (Thomas Yeo et al., 2011). Each of the 96 cortical regions outlined in the Harvard-Oxford atlas was assigned to the network containing the highest number of voxel attributions within that region. Following this, we calculated the mean deviation values across all regions within each network for every patient and compared these values among different MDD subtypes.
   109	
   110	## 2.5. Correlation analysis between subtype-specific alterations and cognitive functions
   111	
   112	We  investigated  the  relationship  between  the  spatial  patterns  of subtype-specific  deviations  and  cognitive  processes  (Fig.  1E).  Neurosynth (https://neurosynth.org/) is a platform that synthesizes results from thousands of fMRI studies, providing a quantitative representation of how specific brain regions/voxels correlate with neuropsychological processes in multitasking fMRI (Yarkoni et al., 2011). Given our focus on cognitive functions reported by Neurosynth, we selected 117 cognitive terms  from  the  Cognitive  Atlas  (https://www.cognitiveatlas.org/),  a publicly accessible ontology in cognitive science that standardizes and integrates  cognitive concepts (Poldrack et al., 2011). All these terms were also represented in Neurosynth, ensuring a comprehensive cognitive analysis.
   113	
   114	To construct the region-by-cognitive function matrix, we adopted the same brain atlas as that used for the deviation maps to guarantee consistency  across  analyses.  We  then  segmented  the  Neurosynth  metaanalytic task activation maps for all selected cognitive terms into 96 distinct regions, where the value assigned to each region corresponded to the mean activation across all voxels within that specific brain area. After excluding cognitive terms that exhibited zero activation across all regions, we obtained a 96 × 114 matrix, which captured the associations between various cognitive functions and the segmented brain regions.
   115	
   116	Subsequently, for each subtype, we conducted a partial least squares (PLS)  regression  analysis  to  explore  the  spatial  correlation  between cognitive  functions  and  the  observed  deviations.  Here,  the  cognitive function matrix served as the predictor variable, while the deviation map acted as the response variable. For each statistically significant PLS component, we employed a bootstrapping approach (with 1000 iterations) to assess the variability of the weights assigned to each cognitive term. To refine these weights, we adjusted the original weights through division by their corresponding estimated bootstrapping errors, resulting in corrected weights for all cognitive terms. Finally, we ranked these cognitive terms according to their corrected weights, which indicated their respective contributions to the significant PLS component.
