78:Brain image processing was performed according to a standardized protocol using the DPARSF software (http://www.rfmri.org/). The first ten functional volumes were discarded to allow for signal equilibration and environmental adaptation. The remaining data were corrected for the  time shifts  among different  acquisitions  within each  volume and then realigned to the first volume for head motion correction. The corrected  images  were  normalized  into  standard  Montreal  Neurological Institute space (resampling voxel size = 3 mm × 3 mm × 3 mm). The nuisance  regression  was  conducted  to  remove  Friston-24  motion  parameters, white matter signals, and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) signals. Temporal bandpass filtering was applied at a frequency range of 0.01 to 0.10 Hz.
80:In this study, Pearson correlation analyses were conducted on the average time courses between pairs of 96 brain regions defined by the Harvard-Oxford  atlas,  with  coefficients  normalized  using  Fisher's-z transformation.  This  procedure  generated  a  96 × 96 functional  connectivity  (FC)  matrix  for  each  participant  to  facilitate  subsequent network topological analysis. To assess the robustness of our findings across different brain atlases, we replicated the analysis using the AAL90 atlas (details in Supplementary Materials). Degree centrality measures direct connections between network nodes without prior assumptions, which prevents deviations from actual data caused by subjective selection  of  brain  regions  (Bullmore  and  Sporns,  2009;  Jia  et  al.,  2023). Following previous research, a network sparsity threshold ranging from 0.05 to 0.50, at 0.01 intervals, was adopted (Larivi ` ere  et  al.,  2022). Degree centrality for each brain region was calculated at every sparsity threshold  using  the  Graph  Theoretical  Network  Analysis  Toolbox (GRETNA, http://www.nitrc.org/projects/gretna), with the area under the curve (AUC) across all thresholds defined as a summarized scalar of network  indicators.  AUC  was  chosen  for  further  statistical  analysis because  it  avoided  potential  biases  introduced  by  selecting  a  single threshold (Tan et al., 2023) and had demonstrated considerable sensitivity in detecting topological variations in brain networks (Maas et al., 2022; Yang et al., 2021). Fig. 1 presented the flowchart of our methodology, with Fig. 1A illustrating the process of calculating network degree centrality for each participant.
84:To implement normative modeling, we utilized the PCNtoolkit Python  package,  version  0.28  (Rutherford  et  al.,  2022).  Normative modeling  maps  demographic  or  behavioral  variables  onto  neurobiological variables and provides estimates of variation percentiles within the cohort by modeling the relationship between the two (Segal et al., 2023). In this study, age and sex were mapped onto the degree centrality of brain functional networks. By placing individuals within a normative range,  we  analyzed  and  quantified  each  patient's  deviation  patterns
94:<!-- formula-not-decoded -->
134:Fig. 2. Normative models established based on HCs' degree centrality and individual deviations from normative models in MDD patients. (A) The brain map in the center displays the mean deviation pattern for all MDD patients. The models on either side represent age-related trends in degree centrality across various regions within a single site, alongside the corresponding normative ranges (female MDD: red dots; 95th percentiles: blue dashed lines; 99th percentiles: light blue dashed lines). (B) Cortical maps and brain slice images of spatial overlap quantify the proportion of patients exhibiting positive (left) and negative (right) outliers in each region. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the web version of this article.)
